// Sapphire's default day (M1 W1), compiled into `sapphire_world`: `TodTimeline::default_day`,
// whose module docs say how keys blend. Packs (A4) will carry timelines as content.
//
// - Eight keys: midnight, the blue hour, sunrise, day, afternoon, golden hour, sunset and dusk.
//   Each sets every group, so this can be the base timeline; between keys every group blends
//   linearly in time (colors in OkLab, directions along great circles).
// - 10:00 is exactly the renderer's default look: the sun the sandbox has always drawn with,
//   `SkyGradient::default()` and `PostParams::default()`. A scene that doesn't choose a time
//   starts there, so its frames are what they were before time of day.
// - Directions are the way light travels, from the body into the scene. Looking from the
//   sandbox's camera, the sun rises on the left, passes high on the camera's side (lighting the
//   character from the front), and sets on the right; the moon rises on the left as the sun
//   sets, and sets on the right as it rises. The key light follows the sun by day and the moon
//   at night, turning across the camera's side in the two twilights.
// - A body lights the scene only from above the horizon, so each one's intensity reaches 0 at
//   the key where it reaches the horizon (the sun at 5:00 and 20:00, the moon at 18:45), and
//   stays 0 below it.
// - Characters get a key light of their own, brighter than the world's at night so they read
//   (WuWa fits character brightness across the day); C2 draws it. Until then they are lit by
//   the world's key light.
// - The sky's layers (M1 W2): `discs` are the sun's and moon's discs and glows, apart from the
//   light the bodies give, so a setting sun's disc fades on the horizon after its light, and the
//   moon shows pale as it rises at sunset. Glows are wide and warm at sunrise and sunset and
//   spread along the horizon there; the dawn and dusk keys keep a glow where the sun is below
//   the horizon. `stars` come out through dusk, fullest at midnight. `clouds` are painted in two
//   tones with a rim: white tops over lavender-blue shade by day, peach and gold over violet at
//   sunrise and sunset, dark blue with moonlit rims at night; the cirrus is fullest at sunrise and
//   sunset, where it catches the color.
// - Every number is a starting point for art direction, not a measurement.
